# Revocable Consent and the Cost of Preserving Choice

**E078 · Reserve · Assessment as of October 31, 2026**

Someone lends a pump on terms that allow its return. If the recipient gives up the backup, reclaiming it can leave others without water. A right to refuse may need a maintained alternative—but who should pay for it?

[Read the selected manuscript](../selection-20261018/E078.md) · [Read the original](original.md)

## How the argument reached this point

**September: branch a common draft.** This essay began in the experiment comparing a starting manuscript with one and three critique–revision cycles. The blind selector chose the one-cycle version. All branches remained in the record; that choice does not establish a generally optimal revision count. [Branching methods](../../docs/generation.md#six-ways-to-begin).

**October: survive weak panel support, then justify the bill.** E078 stayed in contention despite support from only one of 23 midterm profile reviewers. A midterm R1 became its working text, but further work asked why preserving an exit should be charged to a particular party. The October 17 revision distinguished unilateral changes from shared ones and allowed the burden of sacrifice to affect allocation. A separate reviewer accepted the bounded justification. [Preparation decision](../../evidence/final-selection/updates/20261018/preselection/DECISIONS.json).

**October 19–31: keep a disputed route open.** It received a wildcard reading place and later a reserve recommendation. The selected version is the October 17 revision, not the older midterm R1. Fair starting rights, mixed changes and excessive costs remain unresolved. [Selection](../../docs/selection.md).

## What to look for while reading

Separate the need to preserve a usable exit from the further question of who must finance it. The second claim requires more than the first.

